WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.870
Drake's latest album, "Scorpion,"

00:00:01.870 --> 00:00:04.240
debuted at number one
on the Billboard charts

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.180
and had one of the biggest
weeks ever for a hip-hop album.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.030
But to listen to the entire project,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.220
you're going to have to
dedicate a lot of time.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.970
It's Drake's longest album.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:15.130
It's 25 tracks and has a running time

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.150
of one hour and 30 minutes.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:20.040
But Drake isn't the only
artist with a bloated album.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.100
The deluxe edition of Chris Brown's latest

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.490
was a 57-track behemoth
that ran for a solid

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.640
three hours and 18 minutes.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.910
That's longer than "The Two Towers."

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.500
Migos followed a similar
path with "Culture II,"

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.430
which had nearly twice
the amount of tracks

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.360
as their previous album, "Culture."

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.440
But these lengthier albums
aren't just a coincidence.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.883
It might just be a response
to the way we consume music.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.600
To understand, you're
going to have to look

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.840
at streaming services.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.280
Spotify and Apple Music have completely

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.770
changed the music industry.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:58.240
In 2017, 54% of music consumption

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.030
came from streaming platforms,

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.680
and streaming generated $3.9 billion.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.770
But the Billboard charts
have had a hard time

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.580
trying to figure out how to count streams

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.900
in an industry that was once dominated

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.600
by physical album sales.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.210
In 2014, people sat around,
debated, deliberated,

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:18.883
and ultimately decided on a way
to quantify streaming music:

00:01:18.883 --> 00:01:22.330
1,500 streamed songs
counted as one album sale.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.140
Previously, Billboard didn't
take streaming into account.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.740
It only counted the sales of full albums.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.640
This caused a huge shakeup
in the music industry.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.870
Record labels and artists quickly realized

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:35.170
that more tracks equals more streams,

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.710
and that ultimately equals more money.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.230
Artists like Eminem,
Post Malone, and Future

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.490
all held top stops on the
hip-hop charts in 2017,

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.570
with albums that averaged
at least 18 tracks.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.800
Suddenly an individual
track could help an artist

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.650
reach number one on the Billboard charts

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.800
without selling any physical albums,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.000
which was hugely beneficial for hip-hop,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.040
because streaming made up 89%

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.180
of hip-hop consumption in 2017.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:00.510
That's one of the reasons Drake

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.370
might have added "Hotline
Bling" onto "Views."

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:04.930
By adding that one track,

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:07.830
Drake almost guaranteed his
album would go platinum.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.460
It had 400 million
streams that converted to

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:15.460
267,000 streaming albums before
"Views" was even released.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.080
So if an artist has a
massive hit like "One Dance"

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.420
or "God's Plan," it can help
turn an album into a hit.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.190
Streaming listeners quickly pluck

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:24.860
their favorite tracks from albums,

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.790
put them on playlists, and
then reject the leftovers.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.110
In 2015, a research company found that 60%

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.920
of streaming service listeners
only listen to albums

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.310
a few times, because they're
discovering so much new music.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:40.140
So the more tracks you have,
the higher chance you have

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.710
of making a song that lands on a playlist.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.470
But it didn't always work this way.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:47.410
Music was once dominated by
different physical formats.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.840
Vinyl, cassettes, and
CDs all had restrictions

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.580
that dictated an album's length.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.170
But no matter how long
or short an album was,

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:58.600
if one album was purchased
that meant one unit was sold.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.830
But that same rule doesn't apply anymore.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.347
Streaming is the dominant
format, which means

00:03:03.347 --> 00:03:06.450
the 25 tracks on "Scorpion"
make a lot more sense.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.650
You could argue that
Drake is a prolific artist

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.610
who enjoys making long albums,

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.170
or he's an incredible businessman

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.090
who understands his audience's habits.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.940
But no matter how you
feel about streaming,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.550
it's completely altered
the way we listen to music

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:22.260
and the way music is made,
for better or for worse.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.920
Artists are still trying to experiment

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.000
and find their footing
on these new platforms.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.540
Kanye West took the opposite
approach from Drake,

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:29.910
releasing a bunch of albums

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.750
that were only seven tracks long.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.480
Some have argued that the shorter length

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.650
helps with the album's replay value,

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.520
although you'll need to
listen to Kanye's album

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.173
a lot more times to reach 1,500 streams.

